From 24b6a305317dc5baaffd3885685e6d2cddb27f2a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jared Wyles <jared.wyles@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 12:25:11 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 34/34] skippy: sample a wave through a configurable executor

Concurrent sampling started one thread per request for every decode step and
joined them before returning: a thread create and join per token per request,
with no bound on how many ran at once.

How a wave is sampled is now a skippy_sample_executor, chosen by the new
`sample_threads` runtime setting:

  0  the calling thread plus helpers from a shared pool, as many as the wave
     and the hardware allow (the default)
  1  the calling thread alone
  N  the pool, N threads at most

The pool (skippy_task_pool) starts a helper the first time a run can use one,
up to one per spare hardware thread, and reuses them for every step, so a wide
machine serving many lanes gets as many samplers as it has requests and a small
one starts none it cannot use. A task's exception reaches the caller. The
wave's trace span names the executor.

ABI 0.1.73: skippy_runtime_config gains sample_threads.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
